WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:04.440
- I agree, I feel after
reading this paper,

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:06.270
everybody's sort of pushing out,

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:08.920
you need to test, test
and trace trace, right?

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:12.080
And it seems like we're beyond the point,

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:16.077
potentially beyond the point
of tracing at this point.

00:00:16.077 --> 00:00:21.077
But is that like did...

00:00:23.090 --> 00:00:25.460
So we were thinking about
a place like New Zealand

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:27.760
and I don't know if this
is outside of the realm

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:32.760
of this paper, where they
are they're saying that

00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:37.090
they're COVID free.

00:00:37.090 --> 00:00:39.810
And is it because they did
these types of efforts,

00:00:39.810 --> 00:00:43.493
from the very beginning, if you know?

00:00:47.692 --> 00:00:49.090
- I'm not sure.

00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:51.600
I think it is partially because

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:54.060
they had stricter lockdown measures

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:55.890
and they made sure
people were wearing masks

00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:57.280
and they actually shut everything down.

00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:58.740
Cause what happened to the U.S. is,

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:01.015
we didn't actually shut down.

00:01:01.015 --> 00:01:03.570
Our testing capacity is very limited.

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:06.190
And part of how contact tracing works.

00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:07.690
Cause you need to be able to test people

00:01:07.690 --> 00:01:09.780
to say this person has COVID

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:11.803
so that you can actually trace contacts.

00:01:14.983 --> 00:01:15.816
- [Anna] Yeah, I think-

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:18.840
- Sorry, go ahead Anna.

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:19.673
- [Anna] Oh, no problem.

00:01:19.673 --> 00:01:21.050
I just, I read an interesting article

00:01:21.050 --> 00:01:22.730
about contact tracing efforts

00:01:22.730 --> 00:01:25.580
that are currently ongoing in Utah.

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:27.290
Was it Utah Devany?

00:01:27.290 --> 00:01:29.060
- I think so.

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:31.510
- [Anna] Yeah, and I think
it's just sort of interesting

00:01:31.510 --> 00:01:34.530
to think about how in the U.S.,

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:38.370
I think the U.S. is very
heterogeneous, right?

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:40.520
We have a lot of different
States with different approaches,

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:42.610
and I think there are
still places in the U.S.

00:01:42.610 --> 00:01:46.263
where the numbers are
still relatively low.

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:52.190
And so I think that's not
to say that contact tracing

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:56.453
may not have any utility
in certain places.

00:01:57.790 --> 00:01:59.390
I think especially cause,

00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:01.030
as we've learned more about the disease,

00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:06.030
like it seems like R0 is
not 3.5, it's a bit lower.

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:11.420
And I think some of the
scenarios we mapped out here

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:12.993
were a little bit extreme.

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:18.623
But yeah, I think it sort of all,

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:22.320
it really seems like
it's been varying a lot,

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:24.560
like country to country and place to place

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:28.690
about what techniques are
really, really feasible

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:30.403
in different situations.

00:02:33.070 --> 00:02:38.070
- So I was curious, my husband
who also does math models,

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:41.150
but within cancer has to defend his work

00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:43.794
I think a lot to biologists

00:02:43.794 --> 00:02:47.140
and people who don't
necessarily understand his work.

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:50.770
But is there a way to kind
of help people understand

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:53.680
where these kinds of
mathematical models fit

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:58.170
within understanding disease

00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:01.440
kind of particularly these
kinds of infectious diseases

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:04.160
and how you then compare afterwards to say

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:07.203
yes, this was a legitimate
model for this disease.

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:15.310
- [Anna] Devany do you
want to start with this one

00:03:15.310 --> 00:03:17.080
and I can chime in.

00:03:17.080 --> 00:03:17.913
- Sure.

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:22.610
So the issue with models,

00:03:22.610 --> 00:03:26.800
like all of your teachers
that teach you how to model

00:03:28.630 --> 00:03:30.950
discuss is that all models are wrong

00:03:30.950 --> 00:03:33.250
because all models are
an over simplification.

00:03:36.630 --> 00:03:41.100
It's hard to know I think
how accurate a model is

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:44.310
if you're not actually
implementing all the parameters,

00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:46.390
like for this model, for instance,

00:03:46.390 --> 00:03:48.080
like this was based on

00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:51.830
information that turned
out not to be true,

00:03:51.830 --> 00:03:54.960
at least in the U.S. a
lot of, all my research

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:58.320
and reading has been on stuff
that's happening in the U.S.

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:00.320
because this was assuming

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:02.320
that we would have like
a smaller population

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:04.970
and there'd be one or two three four.

00:04:04.970 --> 00:04:06.520
Like a limited number of people

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:07.670
who'd come in with the virus.

00:04:07.670 --> 00:04:09.878
And in the U.S. at least it seemed like

00:04:09.878 --> 00:04:11.703
the number was much, much higher.

00:04:14.376 --> 00:04:15.603
So I think that,

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:21.890
it's less about seeing
whether or not the model

00:04:21.890 --> 00:04:26.570
is a good model and tuning
the parameters of the model

00:04:26.570 --> 00:04:28.410
in order to better match reality

00:04:28.410 --> 00:04:32.060
or putting in other pieces

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:34.980
like maybe this person
goes into isolation,

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:36.610
but they occasionally
come out of their house.

00:04:36.610 --> 00:04:38.040
And how do you model that?

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:41.660
Now they have a higher
infectivity when they leave

00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:43.760
and you have to account for that.

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:46.810
But I think it's about figuring
out what to account for,

00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:49.480
and it really depends a lot on the data

00:04:49.480 --> 00:04:50.580
that you're gathering.

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:55.260
Cause that is what informs
the creation of your model

00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:56.423
in the first place.

00:04:57.580 --> 00:05:02.090
I'll say I'm not an expert
on pandemic modeling.

00:05:02.090 --> 00:05:07.090
I'm a biophysicist, so I,
everything with a grain of salt

00:05:08.420 --> 00:05:10.663
get actual information from experts.

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:16.010
But as someone who's built a
bunch of computational models,

00:05:16.010 --> 00:05:18.640
it really, really depends
on the data that you get

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:23.640
and the effectiveness you
have of gathering the data.

00:05:25.832 --> 00:05:30.832
- [Anna] I think just as an
individual, looking at models

00:05:33.201 --> 00:05:36.370
is a helpful practice to think...

00:05:36.370 --> 00:05:40.090
It forces you to think
really specifically about

00:05:40.090 --> 00:05:44.080
what are the different factors
that could have an effect

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:48.033
since you have to
explicitly list them out.

00:05:49.170 --> 00:05:51.590
And so even just like, as a
person reading this paper,

00:05:51.590 --> 00:05:53.040
sometimes you come across factors

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:54.820
that you hadn't thought of yourself,

00:05:54.820 --> 00:05:57.257
or people make an assumption and you say,

00:05:57.257 --> 00:05:58.527
"Oh, that assumption isn't,

00:05:58.527 --> 00:06:00.240
"I don't think that's a good assumption."

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:04.003
And it forces you to think
more about why that's the case.

00:06:05.010 --> 00:06:08.700
I think doing this work early
at the start of the pandemic

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:09.533
can be helpful

00:06:09.533 --> 00:06:13.250
cause you're sort of exploring
a range of possibilities.

00:06:13.250 --> 00:06:14.870
And then once you've laid the groundwork,

00:06:14.870 --> 00:06:17.500
as you get more accurate information,

00:06:17.500 --> 00:06:20.740
you can feed that into
these existing models.

00:06:20.740 --> 00:06:24.563
So hopefully get slightly
more informed not predictions,

00:06:29.130 --> 00:06:30.270
cause it's not really predicting

00:06:30.270 --> 00:06:35.270
exactly what's gonna happen,
but just more informed models,

00:06:39.740 --> 00:06:42.577
more informed pictures of
what might be possible.

00:06:42.577 --> 00:06:45.770
And I think also really looking at like

00:06:45.770 --> 00:06:50.770
the role of different variables
compared to each other.

00:06:51.220 --> 00:06:53.320
I think one thing that
happens when people run models

00:06:53.320 --> 00:06:57.640
like this is that they'll see,
they'll test the effect of,

00:06:57.640 --> 00:06:58.660
especially when you can vary

00:06:58.660 --> 00:07:01.860
two different parameters at once.

00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:06.860
You might see that, I
can, changing parameter A,

00:07:06.940 --> 00:07:10.320
has a much bigger effect
than changing parameter B.

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:13.000
Even if on their own,
they each have an effect.

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:15.320
There might be a much bigger
effect contribution from one

00:07:15.320 --> 00:07:16.810
than the other, and that can help you then

00:07:16.810 --> 00:07:18.193
sort of tease apart.

00:07:19.810 --> 00:07:24.810
If approach one seems to
have a much more impact,

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:27.210
much greater impact than approach two,

00:07:27.210 --> 00:07:29.420
and then maybe it's worth
fueling our resources

00:07:29.420 --> 00:07:33.043
into approach one rather than
worrying about approach two.

00:07:34.170 --> 00:07:35.670
- Yeah and just for reference.

00:07:35.670 --> 00:07:38.320
So most of my husband David's work

00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:41.100
is actually talking to biologists

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:43.850
and getting the parameters
you were just talking about.

00:07:47.802 --> 00:07:49.730
- And it seems like those parameters

00:07:49.730 --> 00:07:52.050
it's like you only know what you know.

00:07:52.050 --> 00:07:55.210
And so there's always
something that you don't know

00:07:55.210 --> 00:07:56.923
that you're potentially missing.

00:07:57.910 --> 00:08:01.130
And so I imagine it's
complicated and hard,

00:08:01.130 --> 00:08:04.050
but I also feel like there's
so much value in beauty

00:08:04.050 --> 00:08:07.990
and Joanne threw a comment in about

00:08:07.990 --> 00:08:10.120
the beauty of a model that
you can plug in the values

00:08:10.120 --> 00:08:11.770
based on new information.

00:08:11.770 --> 00:08:16.770
And so yeah, I'm grateful for
modeling even though I think,

00:08:18.460 --> 00:08:20.390
you're probably right in saying Devany

00:08:20.390 --> 00:08:22.077
and everybody who teaches modeling,

00:08:22.077 --> 00:08:24.740
"all models wrong", but
it's the best we have

00:08:24.740 --> 00:08:26.470
at this point in time.

00:08:26.470 --> 00:08:31.330
So we have a question
from Stephanie that says,

00:08:31.330 --> 00:08:34.450
did the paper discuss anything
about how long an interaction

00:08:34.450 --> 00:08:36.980
between people had to be worth,

00:08:36.980 --> 00:08:40.000
had to take place in
order to be worth tracing?

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:43.257
Like if somebody was at the grocery store,

00:08:43.257 --> 00:08:45.760
and checked out with a cashier

00:08:45.760 --> 00:08:48.840
versus two friends
catching up for 15 minutes.

00:08:48.840 --> 00:08:52.850
Is there a difference or
in like how we're thinking

00:08:52.850 --> 00:08:54.543
about that for contact tracing.

00:08:57.240 --> 00:09:02.240
- [Anna] So I think in practice,
definitely in this paper,

00:09:02.330 --> 00:09:04.150
no, correct me if I'm wrong Devany.

00:09:04.150 --> 00:09:07.459
But I think they modeled
every contact as identical.

00:09:07.459 --> 00:09:09.000
- [Devany] Yeah.

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:10.800
- [Anna] But I know in
practice, that's definitely

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:13.100
something that people
are talking a lot about.

00:09:14.680 --> 00:09:15.550
Both in terms of like,

00:09:15.550 --> 00:09:17.910
how likely is it that
the disease is spreading

00:09:17.910 --> 00:09:20.860
in these momentary interactions.

00:09:20.860 --> 00:09:23.110
And also along the axis of how feasible

00:09:23.110 --> 00:09:26.170
is it for us to be able
to trace this person?

00:09:26.170 --> 00:09:30.080
Cause if you're relying on
people interacted with clearly,

00:09:30.080 --> 00:09:30.913
that's a problem.

00:09:30.913 --> 00:09:32.730
If they're interacting
with people in passing

00:09:32.730 --> 00:09:35.460
at the grocery store other people,

00:09:35.460 --> 00:09:37.860
other countries have gotten around that,

00:09:37.860 --> 00:09:41.060
like in South Korea, by
using digital tracking

00:09:41.060 --> 00:09:44.190
and accessing people's GPS signals

00:09:44.190 --> 00:09:48.170
and credit card information
like the government doing this,

00:09:48.170 --> 00:09:50.030
to be able to see where people were going

00:09:50.030 --> 00:09:51.683
in relation to each other.

00:09:52.680 --> 00:09:53.710
Clearly that's not something

00:09:53.710 --> 00:09:56.350
that's like politically
feasible in the U.S.

00:09:56.350 --> 00:09:58.590
that people wouldn't be comfortable with.

00:09:58.590 --> 00:10:00.870
So I think that sort of hearkens
back to our earlier point

00:10:00.870 --> 00:10:04.730
that I think you need different approaches

00:10:04.730 --> 00:10:05.800
in different locations.

00:10:05.800 --> 00:10:09.260
It's not a one size fits all situation.

00:10:09.260 --> 00:10:11.680
- It feels like there could
be a very slippery slope

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:16.680
if we give access to our
personal data to government

00:10:18.700 --> 00:10:21.090
people it seems like, I don't know,

00:10:21.090 --> 00:10:23.263
I'm not sure it's worth the risk.

00:10:25.112 --> 00:10:26.912
I might, that's my personal opinion,

00:10:28.828 --> 00:10:30.363
but at least at this point in time.

00:10:31.288 --> 00:10:32.130
- [Anna] I think the great thing though,

00:10:32.130 --> 00:10:33.840
is once you have a model like this,

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:37.910
that that is the type of
question that you could then

00:10:37.910 --> 00:10:39.773
build on this model to incorporate.

00:10:40.640 --> 00:10:44.330
So what we presented today
is these are the assumptions

00:10:44.330 --> 00:10:47.543
and the setup that these
researchers decided to go with.

00:10:48.500 --> 00:10:49.800
But this isn't the only way

00:10:49.800 --> 00:10:51.520
that they could have chosen to do this.

00:10:51.520 --> 00:10:56.520
And if you're coding savvy
and downloaded the simulation,

00:10:56.760 --> 00:11:00.980
you could go and, it probably
would be kind of difficult,

00:11:00.980 --> 00:11:04.400
but you could go and add
on some of these components

00:11:04.400 --> 00:11:07.200
to the existing simulation
to see what effect they have.

00:11:08.430 --> 00:11:10.280
- Yeah, you could model things like

00:11:11.873 --> 00:11:14.200
if you have a weak contact,

00:11:14.200 --> 00:11:16.550
the percent chance that you're
gonna pass on the disease,

00:11:16.550 --> 00:11:19.180
it's like halved or quartered
or something like that,

00:11:19.180 --> 00:11:22.370
or you have a strong contact,
it could be go way off.

00:11:22.370 --> 00:11:24.530
And that's sort of how
you could model that.

00:11:24.530 --> 00:11:27.300
But in this model they did
not take that into account,

00:11:27.300 --> 00:11:28.870
but it's a sort of a baseline

00:11:28.870 --> 00:11:31.763
for building more complex
models in the future.

00:11:32.630 --> 00:11:33.463
- [Anna] And in this model,

00:11:33.463 --> 00:11:36.470
they do actually take into
account for certain variables,

00:11:36.470 --> 00:11:37.533
the fact that inter,

00:11:38.866 --> 00:11:40.710
but these are not the
same for every person.

00:11:40.710 --> 00:11:43.670
So we didn't describe this
in detail, but for example,

00:11:43.670 --> 00:11:45.740
for the onset to isolation delay

00:11:46.630 --> 00:11:48.280
they weren't just plugging in three days

00:11:48.280 --> 00:11:50.100
or eight days for every person.

00:11:50.100 --> 00:11:52.990
They actually had like a distribution

00:11:52.990 --> 00:11:57.300
that they sampled from that
was centered at three days

00:11:57.300 --> 00:12:00.390
for the short delay and
centered at eight days

00:12:00.390 --> 00:12:01.680
for the long delay.

00:12:01.680 --> 00:12:05.090
But you could still get like
seven day delay, ten day delay,

00:12:05.090 --> 00:12:08.760
three day delay, for
different interactions

00:12:08.760 --> 00:12:10.040
in the simulation.

00:12:10.040 --> 00:12:11.710
So there is a little bit more complexity

00:12:11.710 --> 00:12:13.023
that you can build in.

00:12:17.380 --> 00:12:22.380
- So Susan wants to know, is
the contact tracing dependent

00:12:22.440 --> 00:12:25.053
on the reporting of the
infected individual?

00:12:28.020 --> 00:12:30.250
- I think it depends
on like Anna was saying

00:12:30.250 --> 00:12:31.350
the country you're in.

00:12:32.340 --> 00:12:34.300
So here, I believe it would be,

00:12:34.300 --> 00:12:37.473
cause I don't think you can
get information like that.

00:12:39.430 --> 00:12:41.650
You can't get someone's
credit card data to figure out

00:12:41.650 --> 00:12:43.090
like what grocery stores they've been to.

00:12:43.090 --> 00:12:45.950
So you'd have to say, "Who
have you been in contact with?"

00:12:45.950 --> 00:12:48.000
And like, do you have their phone number?

00:12:50.128 --> 00:12:54.080
- Tech companies like, sorry, I'm sorry-

00:12:54.080 --> 00:12:55.344
- No, go for it.

00:12:55.344 --> 00:12:57.163
- For your stopping.

00:12:58.530 --> 00:13:01.940
But aren't there, like, does
didn't Apple like have a thing

00:13:01.940 --> 00:13:05.600
where if somebody, somebody can
input themselves as positive

00:13:05.600 --> 00:13:10.600
and if they've come into
proximity of other iPhone users,

00:13:10.620 --> 00:13:12.960
that there would be some alert.

00:13:12.960 --> 00:13:14.579
- Oh, wow.

00:13:14.579 --> 00:13:15.670
I didn't know that.

00:13:15.670 --> 00:13:17.820
- [Anna] I was reading
about this recently.

00:13:19.766 --> 00:13:21.240
I'm not a hundred percent.

00:13:21.240 --> 00:13:24.120
My understanding of
what's happened is that

00:13:24.120 --> 00:13:26.253
Apple and Google actually teamed up.

00:13:27.100 --> 00:13:28.940
And they've built an API

00:13:30.110 --> 00:13:32.350
which is sort of like a data interface

00:13:32.350 --> 00:13:33.973
that you use via the internet.

00:13:35.380 --> 00:13:38.520
And so they created this API.

00:13:38.520 --> 00:13:39.850
That's supposed to be helpful

00:13:39.850 --> 00:13:43.190
in coordinating that type of information.

00:13:43.190 --> 00:13:47.400
But they have not built any
sort of unified app to do this.

00:13:47.400 --> 00:13:49.040
And so they released it to the States

00:13:49.040 --> 00:13:52.180
and States individually
then have the ability

00:13:52.180 --> 00:13:55.060
for the responsibility
to develop their own app

00:13:57.020 --> 00:13:59.170
using this API to do the tracing.

00:13:59.170 --> 00:14:01.380
But I think as someone
mentioned in the comments,

00:14:01.380 --> 00:14:03.870
I think there has been pushback on this.

00:14:03.870 --> 00:14:06.480
And there are a number of
States who have attempted

00:14:06.480 --> 00:14:09.170
to try and do this, but I
think it hasn't actually,

00:14:09.170 --> 00:14:11.910
I don't know if it's actually
been rolled out anywhere.

00:14:11.910 --> 00:14:14.090
I think there have been some issues.

00:14:14.090 --> 00:14:16.730
- It feels like a task
that only Apple or Google

00:14:16.730 --> 00:14:18.440
can actually accomplish.

00:14:18.440 --> 00:14:21.820
I'm not sure, like for example,
New York state has like,

00:14:21.820 --> 00:14:26.220
a whole tech angle or office
that could build things

00:14:26.220 --> 00:14:28.980
like this without at
least on top of the work

00:14:28.980 --> 00:14:29.930
that they already have.

00:14:29.930 --> 00:14:32.530
It seems, it's an interesting idea

00:14:32.530 --> 00:14:36.677
and I'm not familiar with
how that risks privacy,

00:14:36.677 --> 00:14:38.960
but it feels like we
have so much technology

00:14:38.960 --> 00:14:42.490
and so many tech giant that like,

00:14:42.490 --> 00:14:45.180
I wish that we were doing
more to incorporate that

00:14:45.180 --> 00:14:46.603
into everything.

00:14:47.640 --> 00:14:52.310
- [Anna] I think Taiwan I
don't remember the details,

00:14:52.310 --> 00:14:53.660
but I believe that Taiwan

00:14:55.400 --> 00:15:00.170
developed some sort of
app or some sort of system

00:15:00.170 --> 00:15:03.550
that wasn't even, I think
it was more of like a...

00:15:03.550 --> 00:15:04.700
There's sort of a history,

00:15:04.700 --> 00:15:05.990
I think, of the Taiwanese government

00:15:05.990 --> 00:15:07.660
kind of almost
collaborating with different

00:15:07.660 --> 00:15:12.160
like hacker individuals
within the community.

00:15:12.160 --> 00:15:14.250
And so I think this
was another partnership

00:15:14.250 --> 00:15:16.900
kind of similar to that
where it maybe wasn't as like

00:15:18.120 --> 00:15:20.170
government led necessarily,

00:15:20.170 --> 00:15:21.690
but it was sort of this collaboration

00:15:21.690 --> 00:15:22.810
where they put out this call

00:15:22.810 --> 00:15:24.810
and got people to work on this together.

00:15:25.790 --> 00:15:29.860
- I think there is a really
important balance between,

00:15:29.860 --> 00:15:32.040
peoples' privacy concerns,

00:15:32.040 --> 00:15:33.860
using things like Apple and Google

00:15:33.860 --> 00:15:37.200
versus handing it over to the government.

00:15:37.200 --> 00:15:39.570
I don't necessarily feel
comfortable with either personally,

00:15:39.570 --> 00:15:41.610
but the UK decided that they,

00:15:41.610 --> 00:15:44.700
I think Apple and Google offered to make

00:15:44.700 --> 00:15:45.930
this app, they created it.

00:15:45.930 --> 00:15:48.910
And then they said, well, we
don't really want to use that.

00:15:48.910 --> 00:15:51.160
We want to have our own,
except then of course

00:15:51.160 --> 00:15:54.793
they weren't taking advantage
of the technological advances

00:15:54.793 --> 00:15:55.770
that they have.

00:15:55.770 --> 00:15:57.320
And there were the chances that

00:15:58.300 --> 00:16:01.490
because it wasn't a
sanctioned app for these

00:16:01.490 --> 00:16:02.670
particular devices

00:16:02.670 --> 00:16:04.190
that it wasn't gonna be able to keep up,

00:16:04.190 --> 00:16:06.143
like it wasn't gonna ping as often.

00:16:08.370 --> 00:16:10.020
So yeah, it's a mess essentially.

00:16:12.370 --> 00:16:15.090
- Let's take a question
question from Maggie, who

00:16:15.090 --> 00:16:18.100
I'm gonna give a little
shout out to, hello, Maggie.

00:16:18.100 --> 00:16:20.530
Is there any data about the average time

00:16:20.530 --> 00:16:23.430
it takes for someone to
no longer be contagious

00:16:23.430 --> 00:16:25.240
or recover from COVID-19?

00:16:25.240 --> 00:16:28.160
And if so, has there been any
models that take into account

00:16:28.160 --> 00:16:31.410
the average amount of
time spent in isolation?

00:16:31.410 --> 00:16:34.950
- So the answer to that
is yes, we do know.

00:16:34.950 --> 00:16:36.460
I just looked up an actual number

00:16:36.460 --> 00:16:38.210
from the World Health Organization.

00:16:39.730 --> 00:16:41.570
So they found out that
people with mild disease

00:16:41.570 --> 00:16:42.960
recovery times about two weeks,

00:16:42.960 --> 00:16:47.960
which is why like my lab had
someone come in from overseas,

00:16:48.180 --> 00:16:50.680
right at the very
beginning of the pandemic

00:16:50.680 --> 00:16:54.210
and he had to spend two
weeks in total quarantine.

00:16:54.210 --> 00:16:56.260
So the recovery time was about two weeks.

00:16:57.560 --> 00:16:59.570
And incubation time is shorter than that.

00:16:59.570 --> 00:17:02.640
And then people obviously
who get more sick

00:17:02.640 --> 00:17:03.843
it's gonna be longer.

00:17:05.220 --> 00:17:07.773
Generally they recover
within three to six weeks.

00:17:08.770 --> 00:17:12.693
So this is very simple to take
into account in your model.

00:17:13.685 --> 00:17:15.130
What was the actual question?

00:17:15.130 --> 00:17:16.430
Any models that take into account

00:17:16.430 --> 00:17:18.430
the average amount of
time spent in isolation?

00:17:18.430 --> 00:17:22.833
Yeah, so I think the standard
isolation period is two weeks.

00:17:23.760 --> 00:17:27.390
I've seen several
people, I'm in a new lab,

00:17:27.390 --> 00:17:30.610
so a lot of people are
coming in moving around.

00:17:30.610 --> 00:17:33.360
So we've had a bunch of people
have to be in isolation.

00:17:35.540 --> 00:17:38.950
I think Anna and I had a
friend who moved to Anna's lab

00:17:38.950 --> 00:17:40.760
who came in from China

00:17:40.760 --> 00:17:42.900
and she had to spend
two weeks in quarantine

00:17:42.900 --> 00:17:44.330
before she started working.

00:17:44.330 --> 00:17:48.750
So I think that's probably
like their time for isolation.

00:17:48.750 --> 00:17:51.540
Cause seems like that's the standard time.

00:17:51.540 --> 00:17:53.060
- How's that enforced?

00:17:53.060 --> 00:17:55.650
Or is it enforced or is it
sort of like the honor system?

00:17:55.650 --> 00:17:57.050
- I think it's honor system.

00:17:59.720 --> 00:18:02.720
And I think people, well, I'm
pretty sure the people I know

00:18:02.720 --> 00:18:06.100
who've been had to be under quarantine

00:18:06.100 --> 00:18:07.120
or has been pretty careful

00:18:07.120 --> 00:18:08.920
cause a lot of them are
working in hospitals

00:18:08.920 --> 00:18:10.890
cause these are the people, Anna and I-

00:18:16.680 --> 00:18:18.420
- [Anna] A really
interesting little factoid

00:18:18.420 --> 00:18:21.823
that's tangentially related
that I learned is that,

00:18:23.600 --> 00:18:26.270
initially they, I remember
at the start of the pandemic,

00:18:26.270 --> 00:18:30.600
there was some talk about the
people who were in isolation

00:18:31.610 --> 00:18:34.560
using repeat viral testing.

00:18:34.560 --> 00:18:38.103
So the PCR tests to see
if you have virus in you.

00:18:39.570 --> 00:18:41.160
Using like negative,

00:18:41.160 --> 00:18:43.290
they were trying to use a
negative readings on that

00:18:43.290 --> 00:18:46.193
as a criteria for letting
someone out of isolation.

00:18:47.170 --> 00:18:49.710
But what we've learned since then is that

00:18:50.930 --> 00:18:53.760
the virus actually like
particles of the virus

00:18:53.760 --> 00:18:56.630
stay in your system for a very long time,

00:18:56.630 --> 00:18:58.180
much longer than the period

00:18:58.180 --> 00:19:00.950
through which you're actually infectious.

00:19:00.950 --> 00:19:05.901
So you can't use the PCR test really

00:19:05.901 --> 00:19:09.800
to determine when someone
is no longer infectious.

00:19:09.800 --> 00:19:12.450
Because they can keep showing
up positive on that test

00:19:12.450 --> 00:19:16.143
for up to 12 weeks
after they had symptoms.

00:19:17.190 --> 00:19:20.360
But they are not, they're
certainly no longer infectious

00:19:20.360 --> 00:19:23.393
at that point, the actual
infectious period is much shorter.

00:19:24.610 --> 00:19:25.860
- So how do you, I mean,

00:19:25.860 --> 00:19:28.630
do you have to just
lean on antibody levels

00:19:28.630 --> 00:19:33.630
or how do you make that determination?

00:19:33.942 --> 00:19:37.340
- [Anna] Yeah, so the current approach

00:19:38.940 --> 00:19:40.380
at least that I'm familiar with

00:19:40.380 --> 00:19:44.010
and that I believe they're
using in the hospitals

00:19:44.010 --> 00:19:45.680
here in New York or at Mount Sinai

00:19:45.680 --> 00:19:49.693
are 10 days from symptom onset.

00:19:51.930 --> 00:19:55.570
In addition to being three
days without any fever

00:19:55.570 --> 00:19:58.260
or fever reducing medications

00:19:58.260 --> 00:20:01.593
and the complete
alleviation of all symptoms.

00:20:03.430 --> 00:20:07.083
So it's more a symptom
and time-based metrics.

00:20:08.300 --> 00:20:09.133
- Interesting.

00:20:10.860 --> 00:20:15.230
- So tying back to the
talk that our colleague

00:20:15.230 --> 00:20:18.750
Dr. Odaelys Pollard gave
recently on diabetes,

00:20:18.750 --> 00:20:23.650
is there a way to include
diabetes incidents for example,

00:20:23.650 --> 00:20:24.853
in this type of model?

00:20:30.580 --> 00:20:31.413
- I believe so.

00:20:31.413 --> 00:20:35.780
Are we looking at how much
this person is going to suffer

00:20:35.780 --> 00:20:37.513
from the disease when they get it?

00:20:39.640 --> 00:20:41.420
- Well, I think maybe severity.

00:20:41.420 --> 00:20:45.610
I mean aren't diabetic
patients are at higher risk

00:20:45.610 --> 00:20:47.850
for more adverse.

00:20:47.850 --> 00:20:50.313
- Yeah, they're in one
of the high risk groups.

00:20:52.220 --> 00:20:55.540
So this, I think is more modeling spread

00:20:55.540 --> 00:20:57.693
rather than actual severity.

00:20:58.670 --> 00:21:01.670
And the one parameter I
would think that would affect

00:21:01.670 --> 00:21:04.490
the model based on how severe
your symptoms are gonna be

00:21:04.490 --> 00:21:06.740
is the amount of time
that you had the virus.

00:21:07.670 --> 00:21:11.363
That might affect like how long
you need to be in isolation.

00:21:14.060 --> 00:21:15.780
But that's not,

00:21:15.780 --> 00:21:18.810
I don't think that's gonna
really affect the model

00:21:18.810 --> 00:21:20.313
Anna you can correct me.

00:21:21.300 --> 00:21:22.900
- [Anna] I think you're right
in saying that this model

00:21:22.900 --> 00:21:25.290
is really just modeling spread.

00:21:25.290 --> 00:21:28.360
It's not really looking
at like what's happening

00:21:28.360 --> 00:21:29.710
to the people who get sick.

00:21:31.750 --> 00:21:35.360
So, but there are other models certainly

00:21:35.360 --> 00:21:38.010
that you could incorporate
those sorts of factors into,

00:21:38.010 --> 00:21:43.010
I think the SIR model that
was presented on May 20th

00:21:44.840 --> 00:21:47.820
that one looks at, people being infected

00:21:47.820 --> 00:21:50.020
and then also some
proportion of people being

00:21:50.020 --> 00:21:52.240
either recovering or dying.

00:21:52.240 --> 00:21:55.590
And so I imagine that you
could elaborate on that model

00:21:55.590 --> 00:21:58.550
and incorporate in variables

00:21:58.550 --> 00:22:01.480
like the proportion of the
population that is diabetic

00:22:01.480 --> 00:22:04.370
and looking at the effect
on that sub-population

00:22:04.370 --> 00:22:05.620
within a model like that.

00:22:07.120 --> 00:22:10.030
- I guess there is that
one small subset of people

00:22:10.030 --> 00:22:13.020
who with regards to smokers,

00:22:13.020 --> 00:22:14.430
I don't know if other people read this,

00:22:14.430 --> 00:22:16.670
that smoke has potentially
were less likely

00:22:16.670 --> 00:22:20.530
to get the infection,
but if they did get it,

00:22:20.530 --> 00:22:21.440
they're more likely to have

00:22:21.440 --> 00:22:25.620
very adverse effects of symptoms.

00:22:25.620 --> 00:22:26.560
- That's weird.

00:22:26.560 --> 00:22:27.610
I wonder why that is?

00:22:28.540 --> 00:22:30.880
- Yeah, the thing is we don't
want to encourage people

00:22:30.880 --> 00:22:32.162
to smoke.

00:22:32.162 --> 00:22:34.579
(all laughs)

00:22:37.573 --> 00:22:39.700
But yeah, it feels like that
that could be a parameter

00:22:39.700 --> 00:22:42.490
that then could potentially plug into

00:22:42.490 --> 00:22:44.180
if they're less likely to be infected,

00:22:44.180 --> 00:22:46.080
then they're less likely to spread it.

00:22:46.950 --> 00:22:48.520
- [Anna] And that's a
parameter that definitely

00:22:48.520 --> 00:22:50.920
I could see would vary a
lot country to country.

00:22:52.110 --> 00:22:54.560
And so, if you're, you
can take that into account

00:22:54.560 --> 00:22:57.020
the differences in populations
between different locations

00:22:57.020 --> 00:23:00.656
and that might affect the
sorts of control approaches

00:23:00.656 --> 00:23:02.183
that you might want to take.

00:23:03.260 --> 00:23:05.740
I think with regard to
some of these different,

00:23:05.740 --> 00:23:08.300
these different health considerations.

00:23:08.300 --> 00:23:11.380
I was reading a little bit about how,

00:23:11.380 --> 00:23:13.110
I think initially everyone
was really concerned

00:23:13.110 --> 00:23:16.700
about people with existing
respiratory issues,

00:23:16.700 --> 00:23:18.073
people with asthma.

00:23:19.190 --> 00:23:20.760
But from what I've read,

00:23:20.760 --> 00:23:22.950
it seems like it's turned out to be less,

00:23:22.950 --> 00:23:26.770
those people are not
necessarily as much of a concern

00:23:26.770 --> 00:23:28.250
or at least asthmatic people aren't.

00:23:28.250 --> 00:23:31.300
And that actually, it's
really people with preexisting

00:23:31.300 --> 00:23:33.260
like heart disease and heart conditions

00:23:33.260 --> 00:23:35.800
because actually the virus really,

00:23:35.800 --> 00:23:37.420
there's been a lot of clotting involved

00:23:37.420 --> 00:23:41.473
and a lot of damage to the
endothelial lining I believe.

00:23:42.453 --> 00:23:44.100
And really it's the cardiovascular system

00:23:44.100 --> 00:23:47.320
that really takes a hit in a lot of ways.

00:23:47.320 --> 00:23:49.500
So it's a slightly different
population of people

00:23:49.500 --> 00:23:52.350
we're starting to learn are at risk

00:23:52.350 --> 00:23:54.253
compared to what we initially thought.

00:23:56.790 --> 00:24:00.370
- It's just, it seems like
such a complicated biology

00:24:00.370 --> 00:24:03.200
and to be in it real time

00:24:03.200 --> 00:24:06.350
while also trying to keep our people safe

00:24:06.350 --> 00:24:09.103
is not small feat, I think.

